vendored-2004-headers-beat-reconstructed-2007-ones	0x008654A0	106	identified	THE LOAD-BEARING FIND OF THE RUN, AND IT IS A LAYOUT FACT RATHER THAN A SHAPE ONE. Six GameSpy Chat/Peer translation units lie end to end in 0x00860020..0x00866400 - chatMain.c, peerPing.c, peerPlayers.c, peerRooms.c, peerKeys.c, peerHost.c - all reconstructed from the 2007 SDK because the 2004 carrier ships no chat/ or peer/ module. EVERY peerKeys.c BODY MISSED BY A CONSTANT OFFSET UNTIL THE HEADERS WERE MIXED: the 2007 peerMain.h makes SBServerList and SBQueryEngine POINTER TYPEDEFS, while the VENDORED 2004 serverbrowsing/sb_internal.h makes them STRUCTS BY VALUE. That is 3028 bytes inside piConnection and it moves the watch-key block from +0x0CD8 to +0x18C4. With the 2004 definitions every body went byte-exact. Likewise 2004's chatGlobalCallbacks has FIVE members where 2007 has eight, putting loginType at +0x08A8. THE GENERAL RULE: WHEN RECONSTRUCTING FROM A LATER SDK, TAKE EVERY STRUCT THAT THE VENDORED CONTEMPORARY TREE ACTUALLY DEFINES FROM THE VENDORED TREE - the later text is a guide to CODE, not to LAYOUT. Two further places where retail plainly carries the OLDER text were followed against 2007 rather than with it: ciNickIsValid uses the pre-2004 isdigit/'-' rule that 2007 comments out, and chatConnectDoit passes its serverAddress/port PARAMETERS to ciSocketConnect.
read-rel32-back-to-place-the-next-module	0x00862540	106	tooling	A PLACEMENT TECHNIQUE WORTH PROMOTING, USED WHERE THE USUAL ONES WERE UNAVAILABLE. This range has NO dispatch table - a scan of .rdata and .data for pointers into it returns one hit and that hit is a false positive - so the trick that cracked peerCallbacks.c did not apply. What worked was two things in sequence. FIRST, STRING ANCHORS against the actual SDK text: the IRC command literals NICK/USER/WHO/WHOIS/GETKEY/SETCKEY/CRYPT des place chatMain.c, `PNG` places peerPing.c, and `(END)` - WHICH OCCURS EXACTLY ONCE IN THE WHOLE SDK - places piGetRoomKeysCallbackA and therefore peerKeys.c. SECOND, AND THIS IS THE PART TO REUSE: ONCE ONE TU MATCHES, READ RETAIL'S OWN REL32 DISPLACEMENTS BACK THROUGH THE COMPILED BODY. Its call sites then hand over the addresses of piGetPlayer, piRoomToType, piPingInitPlayer, ciAddCallback_ and ciThink's seven callees FOR FREE, which places the next module without any further guessing. A THIRD CHECK CONFIRMED THE RESULT: each TU lands as a CONTIGUOUS SOURCE-ORDER RUN WITH ZERO GAPS - peerKeys 28 bodies, peerPlayers 27, peerPing 24 - and it is that contiguity, not the bytes, that places the small members a byte comparison could never distinguish.
stlport-is-linked-in-and-its-src-is-not-vendored	0x00840DF0	37	identified	A WHOLE STANDARD LIBRARY IDENTIFIED FROM RETAIL'S OWN RTTI, AND A PROJECT DECISION IT SURFACES. 0x00840DF0..0x008492F0 is the STLport 4.5.3 STATIC LIBRARY - iostreams plus the locale facets - which the game links as its C++ standard library. THE EVIDENCE IS NOT SHAPE: every vftable in the range carries an MSVC RTTI complete-object locator four bytes below it, and the type descriptors read out literally - .?AV?$codecvt@DDH@_STL@@, .?AV?$basic_filebuf@D...@_STL@@, .?AV?$moneypunct@D$00@_STL@@, .?AV?$time_get@D...@_STL@@. IT IS SEVERAL TUs, ONE STLport src/*.cpp PER FACET FAMILY, laid out in link order, and the eight standard stream objects sit at 0x0130BD30 + 0x68*n. THE BLOCKER IS A VENDORING GAP, NOT A CONVERSION PROBLEM: vendor/stlport ships HEADERS ONLY, so everything reachable by explicit instantiation over the headers converted (37 rows, no reconstruction), and the remaining ~77 rows live in src/*.cpp files - num_put_float, num_get_float, locale_impl, iostream, message_facets, facets_byname - THAT ARE NOT IN THE TREE AT ALL. VENDORING STLport's src/ WOULD LIKELY UNLOCK MOST OF THEM; that is a project decision and is recorded here rather than acted on.
a-metric-can-be-right-and-still-mislead	0x00840DF0	3	tooling	A THIRD CORRECTION TO MY OWN FILE-SELECTION TOOL, AND THIS ONE IS SUBTLER THAN THE LAST TWO. After fixing the locality metric to count UNCLAIMED-to-UNCLAIMED calls - the previous version counted calls into already-converted neighbours and so inflated any range with prior work - d_00840df0 scored 36%, the highest available. THE SCORE WAS CORRECT AND THE INFERENCE FROM IT WAS NOT. The file is the STLport library: SEVERAL translation units, one per facet family. The 36% is REAL locality, but it is locality WITHIN EACH OBJECT, not across the file. SO HIGH LOCALITY CONFIRMS THAT SOME COHERENT UNIT EXISTS IN THE RANGE; IT DOES NOT CONFIRM THAT THE RANGE IS ONE UNIT. A file can score well and still need splitting before any layout work begins, which is what both the STLport range and the four-module GameSpy range required. The practical consequence is that a lane should be told to FIND the connected components first and work one, rather than to assume the file is a translation unit - and that is now how the briefs read.
two-rows-backed-out-to-keep-two-checks-honest	0x001BEBB0	2	note	A CONFLICT BETWEEN A LANE'S FINDING AND TWO GATE CHECKS, RESOLVED BY BACKING OUT RATHER THAN BY WIDENING EITHER CHECK. A lane landed Object::friend_notifyOfNewMapBoundary and Object::setLayer, both byte-exact. EACH BROKE A DIFFERENT INVARIANT. The first reads ?ThePartitionManager@@3PAVPartitionManager@@A at a base the rest of the tree does not use, so verify_dir32_consistency reported ONE SYMBOL AT TWO ADDRESSES - the precise hidden-bug signal that check exists for, and not something to whitelist. The second needs a symbols.csv pin for its callee at 0x003E3F70, and adding it made ?removePos@Pathfinder@@QAEXPAVObject@@@Z resolve to FOUR addresses of FOUR DIFFERENT SIZES (13, 163, 424, 266) - pin_consistency's own message says to fix the pin and explicitly NOT to baseline it. THREE OF THOSE FOUR PINS PREDATE THIS RUN, so the ledger's removePos claim was already suspect and the lane's observation that 0x0089F420 takes no argument while 0x003E3F70 takes an Object* is probably right. IT IS STILL NOT A THING TO FIX IN PASSING. Both rows are out, the pin is reverted, and the contradiction is logged for a pass that can weigh all four claims together. A LANE'S GREEN ROW IS NOT WORTH A GATE CHECK'S HONESTY.
minimise-tested-the-directory-not-the-notes	0x008601F0	180	tooling	A BUG IN MY COMMIT TOOLING THAT ABORTED A COMMIT, AND THE FIX IS TO USE THE PROJECT'S OWN TEST INSTEAD OF INVENTING ONE. The minimal-diff rebuild refuses to retire anything that is not a scaffold, which is right - but it decided `is a scaffold` BY DIRECTORY, accepting only Code/gen_asm/. DUMP ROWS ALSO LIVE IN Code/gen_small/dumps_*.cpp, so a perfectly ordinary claim over 0x008601F0 was rejected as landing on a non-scaffold. add_match_batch.py - which the project already trusts to decide what may be retired - tests notes.startswith("gen-dump"), and that is now what the rebuild tests too. THE GENERAL POINT: WHEN TWO TOOLS MUST AGREE ON A CATEGORY, ONE SHOULD ASK THE OTHER'S QUESTION RATHER THAN APPROXIMATE IT. A related slip in the same session: I ran `git stash` to test the gate against HEAD while FOUR LANES' UNCOMMITTED WORK sat in the tree, and the pop then failed on a regenerated artifact. Nothing was lost - the stash entry survives a failed pop - but testing HEAD by stashing live work is the wrong instrument; compare against `git show HEAD:file` instead.
