multiprecision-addsub-operand-materialisation	0x0080FDD0	149	note	BOTH MULTI-PRECISION ADD AND SUBTRACT RESIST FOR ONE SHARED REASON, which narrows the earlier note on 0x0080FD30 considerably. 0x0080FDD0 is the subtract and fails the same way: retail loads the RIGHT operand into ecx first, then the LEFT into eax, then `sub eax,ecx` - while my build of the identical expression `*a - *b - borrow` loads LEFT first and emits `sub ecx,eax`. Same value, mirrored register allocation. The add at 0x0080FD30 shows the same right-operand-first loading. TEN SPELLINGS HAVE NOW BEEN TRIED ACROSS THE TWO bodies - every associativity, both operand orders, compound assignment, explicit parentheses, casts - and all produce left-first. So this is NOT an expression-shape problem and further guessing at C is wasted: the two differ only in which operand MSVC materialises first, and my compiler invocation consistently picks the other one. WHAT THIS RULES IN: something about the declaration rather than the statement - the parameter types, the pointer const-ness, or a helper/macro that presents one operand differently. WHAT IT RULES OUT: the loop structure, the pointer walk, the limb width and the carry extraction, all of which match byte for byte in both bodies. Four neighbours in the same file (0x0080FE70, 0x0080F200, 0x0080F300, 0x0080FED0) matched first or second try, so the flags and the representation are correct.
