gz-alone-is-not-rtcu-and-that-helper-needs-no-pin	0x007EDE00	1	tooling	TWO THINGS, ONE OF WHICH CORRECTS A COMMIT MESSAGE I ALREADY PUSHED. FIRST: /GZ BY ITSELF DOES NOT TURN ON UNINITIALISED-USE CHECKING in this toolchain, contrary to what its documentation implies. Retail's 0x007EDE00 tracks two locals with guard bytes and calls __RTC_UninitUse on them, because both are assigned only inside a while loop whose body may not run, so the return expression's reads are not dominated by an assignment. Compiled with /Od /GZ /MD I got no tracking at all and a frame FOUR BYTES SHORT; adding /RTCu alongside /GZ reproduced retail exactly. IMPORTANTLY IT IS SAFE TO ADD TU-WIDE: 0x007EDB10 in the same translation unit has locals that ARE all dominated, attracts no checks, and stayed byte-exact across the flag change. So the rule is to reach for /RTCu whenever a body reads a local that is only assigned inside a conditional or a loop, and not to fear it for the rest of the file. SECOND, THE CORRECTION: my commit message for that landing says the helper was 'pinned at 0x009F7AE7'. IT WAS NOT AND DID NOT NEED TO BE. __RTC_UninitUse is ALREADY A CONVERTED ROW at that address, 166 bytes, out of the vendored msvc71 libc.lib (error.obj). The pin I added was a duplicate, was discarded when a push race reset the tree, and the landing verified byte-exact twice without it. CHECK functions.csv AS WELL AS symbols.csv BEFORE PINNING A CRT HELPER -- the vendored CRT already supplies many of them.
a-ternary-mask-compiles-branchless-at-od	0x007EDE00	1	tooling	A CODEGEN SHAPE WORTH KNOWING BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS SPELLING IS ONE BYTE WRONG. Retail ends this body with xor eax,eax / cmp / setne al / DEC EAX / and eax,[mid]. The arithmetic reading of that is (res != 0) - 1, and writing exactly that gives a three-byte SUB EAX,1 where retail has the one-byte DEC. What actually produces DEC is the TERNARY: ( res ? 0 : -1 ) & mid. MSVC 7.1 contracts the shape x ? 0 : -1 into setcc plus dec WITH OPTIMISATION OFF -- there is no jump anywhere in the sequence, at /Od, which is not what one expects from this compiler. Six other spellings were tried and all lose: (res!=0)-1, !!res-1, (res!=0)+~0, -1+(res!=0), (unsigned)(res!=0)-1 and (res!=0)-1u all give SUB; -(res==0) gives SETE+NEG; ~(unsigned)(res!=0) gives NOT. THE GENERAL POINT: a branchless mask in the bytes does not mean the source was written as arithmetic. Where a mask is one instruction shorter than the arithmetic that computes the same value, try the conditional expression first.
string-h-cannot-be-included-in-an-md-tu	0x007EC780	1	tooling	A ONE-LINE CHANGE THAT TAKES A TU FROM 8/8 TO 1/8, AND IT IS THE INCLUDE RATHER THAN THE CODE. Under /MD the CRT headers declare memcpy, memmove and memcmp __declspec(dllimport), so every call compiles to an INDIRECT ff 15 straight through the IAT. Retail calls the import STUB with a DIRECT e8. Adding #include <string.h> to an otherwise byte-exact /MD translation unit therefore breaks every mem* call site in it at once; plain prototypes (void * __cdecl memcpy(void *, const void *, unsigned int) and friends) reproduce retail. Note the implication about the ORIGINAL source: the translation unit retail was built from did not see <string.h> either, which is a fact about EA's code and not just about how to satisfy the gate. Two stubs were named while landing this, both read from their IAT slots: _memcmp at 0x009F75D6 (slot 0x01359454, one entry before _memcpy in an alphabetical import-name table) and _memmove at 0x009F6EC6 (slot 0x0135945C, which an Open-BFME5 note on STLport _M_fill_insert reads the same way).
